  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Ramesh.N vs K.S.R.T.C on 15 February, 2025 Scch-21 1 Mvc No.3217/2023 Kabc0B0008322023 In The Court Of Xvii Addl. Judge, Court Of Small Causes & Member, M.A.C.T, Mayo Hall Unit, Bengaluru (Scch-21) Present: Vijaykumar S. Hiremath, Llb. Xvii Addl. Judge, Court of Small Causes & Member, M.A.C.T., Bengaluru. Dated this the 15th Day of February-2025 M.V.C. No.3217/2023 Petitioner/s: 1. Ramesh. N S/o Nanjundappa, Aged 33 years, R/at Channenahalli, Kadalaveni, Gowribidanur Taluk, Chikkaballapura-561210. (By Sri. B. Y. Ravi, Adv.,) Vs. Respondent/s: 1. The Divisional Manager, Karnataka State Road Transport Corporation, K. H. Road, Shanthi Nagar, Bengaluru-560 027.",
    "facts": "The petitioner has filed this petition under Section 166 of Motor Vehicle Act, claiming compensation of Rs.20,00,000/- for the accidental injuries sustained by him in a road traffic accident took place on 14.01.2023. 2. The Brief facts of the petitioner's case is as under: It is the case of the petitioner that on 14.01.2023 at about 9.00 p.m., petitioner was traveling as a passenger in KSRTC Bus bearing Reg. No. KA-40-F-1336, which was driven by its driver at a very high speed and in a rash and negligent manner endangering to human life, on NH-44 road, at Maruru cross, Rapthadu Mandal, dashed against the road side divider. Due to the tremendous impact, the petitioner fell down from the seat and sustained multiple all over the body. Immediately, the petitioner was shifted to Government General Hospital, Anantapur, wherein first aid treatment was given and thereafter petitioner was shifted to Omega Multi Speciality Hospital, Bengaluru, wherein he was treated as inpatient and petitioner has spent huge amount towards medical expenses, Conveyance, food, transport and other charges. The petitioner further submitted that, prior to the accident he was heal and healthy and aged about 33 years, the petitioner doing Coolie work and earning a sum of Rs.25,000/- per month. Due to the injuries sustained in the accident the petitioner has become permanently disabled and lost his future income. Respondent being the owner cum internal insurer of the offending vehicle is liable to pay the compensation to the petitioner. Hence, the petitioner has filed this petition for a compensation of Rs.20,00,000/- with interest. 3. After service of notice, the respondent has appeared through his counsel and filed the written statement by denying the contents of the claim petition as false and contended that the petition is neither maintainable in law nor on facts and also denying the rash and negligent driving of the offending vehicle. He also denied the age, occupation and income of the petitioner. This respondent further submitted that, on the date and time of the alleged accident in question was on its schedule trip from Bangalore to Ananthapur and the bus was being driven by its driver slowly and cautiously on the left side of the road by observing all the traffic rules and regulations, while so proceeding on Bangalore-Ananthapur main road at Maruru cross at that point of time a motorcyclist abruptly darted the main road from the cross road, observing the same the driver of the bus, in order to avoid the bus being dashed against the motorcycle immediately stopped the bus and which has resulted in the minor incident. But no passengers of the bus sustained injuries. As such, for the alleged incident unknown motorcyclist was wholly and solely responsible but the driver of the bus in question was no way responsible. However, the petitioner and police was unable to tract out the motorcycle and falsely implicated the bus in question. By these reasons, it is prayed to dismiss the petition. In order to prove the case of the petitioner, he has got himself examined as P.W.1 and got marked documents at Ex.P.1 to P.15 and examined Dr. Nagaraj. B. N as PW.2 and got marked the documents at Ex.P.16 and Ex.P.17 and closed their side. On the other hand, respondent examine two witnesses as RW.1 and RW.2 and got marked the documents at Ex.R.1 and Ex.R.2.",
    "arguments": "16. In this case, learned counsel for respondent argued that, accident is not taken place due to the negligent act of the driver of the offending vehicle. Further, he argued that, petitioner has filed complaint after 14 days of the accident. This view of this tribunal receives support from decisions of Hon'ble Apex Court as well as High Court which are reported in, 2022 ACJ 2482 between United India Insurance Co. Ltd., Vs. Sahanaz Bibi and others, 2022 ACJ 170 between Venkatesha K. N Vs. Thipregowda and others and 2011 ACJ 2350 between Bangalore Metropolitan Transport Corporation Vs. Pullaiah . 18. In a claim of compensation U/s.166 of M.V.Act 1988, the claimant has to prove the incident only on preponderance of probabilities and standard of proof beyond reasonable doubt is not required as held by Honble Apex Court in Kusum and others Vs. Satveer and others reported in 2011 SAR (CIVIL) 319. 29. INTEREST: As far as awarding of interest on the compensation amount is concerned, I have relied on a decision reported in M.F.A. No.100090 of 2014 in between Vijay Ishwar Jadhav and others V/s The Divisional Manager, Bajaj Allianz General Insurance Co. Ltd, wherein the Hon'ble High Court of Karnataka has awarded interest at the rate of 6%.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "This view of this tribunal receives support from decisions of Hon'ble Apex Court as well as High Court which are reported in, 2022 ACJ 2482 between United India Insurance Co. Ltd., Vs. Sahanaz Bibi and others, 2022 ACJ 170 between Venkatesha K. N Vs. Thipregowda and others and 2011 ACJ 2350 between Bangalore Metropolitan Transport Corporation Vs. Pullaiah .",
    "respondent_arguments": "16. In this case, learned counsel for respondent argued that, accident is not taken place due to the negligent act of the driver of the offending vehicle. Further, he argued that, petitioner has filed complaint after 14 days of the accident.",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "18. In a claim of compensation U/s.166 of M.V.Act 1988, the claimant has to prove the incident only on preponderance of probabilities and standard of proof beyond reasonable doubt is not required as held by Honble Apex Court in Kusum and others Vs. Satveer and others reported in 2011 SAR (CIVIL) 319. 29. INTEREST: As far as awarding of interest on the compensation amount is concerned, I have relied on a decision reported in M.F.A. No.100090 of 2014 in between Vijay Ishwar Jadhav and others V/s The Divisional Manager, Bajaj Allianz General Insurance Co. Ltd, wherein the Hon'ble High Court of Karnataka has awarded interest at the rate of 6%."
  },
